*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !create_user(username)
*
*    Create a Windows local user.
*
*    This function sets the STATUS() value to 0 if successful and returns true
*    Otherwise sets status to the os status and returns false
*
* 0 success
* 1 the account could not be created - it probably already exists
* 2 the name is not a valid OS user name
* 5 access denied: SD is not running elevated.  This is the usual failure.
*
*    THE ACCOUNT IS CREATED DISABLED AND WITH NO PASSWORD, and SET_PASSWD
*    turns it on.  Windows will not create an account without either a
*    password or -NoPassword, and an enabled account with a blank password
*    would be a real hole for as long as it lasted.  Disabled cannot be logged
*    in to at all, so the window is closed instead of being made short.  If
*    SET_PASSWD is never run, what is left behind is unusable rather than
*    open, which is the right way round.
*
*    ELEVATION.  Creating a local user requires an elevated token.  An
*    ordinary SD session does not have one - a UAC-filtered token carries
*    Administrators as "deny only" - so this returns status 5 and the caller
*    reports it.  Measured on 14 Aug 2026: net localgroup ... /add answers
*    "System error 5 has occurred.  Access is denied."  Section 5.7's service
*    model is the real answer; until then SD has to be started elevated for
*    account creation to work.
*
*    The elevation test is made explicitly rather than inferred from a failure
*    message, because failure messages are localised and exit codes from
*    New-LocalUser are not specific.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function create_user(username)
$catalog !create_user

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

user_created = @false
set.status 0
* Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
* Use shared !valid_os_name before OS.EXECUTE (phase 2).
* os.name.chars = '...'
* if username = '' or convert(os.name.chars, '', username) # '' or len(username) > 32 then
if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
   set.status 2
   return user_created
end

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - was: OS.EXECUTE "sudo useradd -m " : username
*   The script is wrapped in single quotes for bash, so every $ and backslash
*   in it is literal, and it uses double quotes for its own strings.  The name
*   has been through !valid_os_name, which permits letters, digits, dot,
*   underscore and hyphen only, so it cannot escape the quoting.
   q = char(39)

   ps = 'if (-not (New-Object Security.Principal.WindowsPrincipal('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent())).IsInRole('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsBuiltInRole]::Administrator)) { exit 5 }; '
   ps := 'try { New-LocalUser -Name "' : username : '" -NoPassword -Disabled '
   ps := '-Description "SD account" -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null } '
   ps := 'catch { exit 1 }; exit 0'

   os.execute 'powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command ' : q : ps : q : ' >/dev/null 2>&1'
* --------------------
stat = OS.ERROR()
if stat = 0 then
  user_created = @TRUE
end else
  set.status stat
end

return user_created
end

